{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>First reading</b><br>אגוז (walnut). Known in the language of the Sages of blessed memory:" "ואבענבים. The early-ripening fruit that comes first, like ואנביה שגיא (\"and its grapes are great\")." "הנצו. Like עלתה נצה (\"the blossom went up\")." "<b>Second reading</b><br>And I went down to the walnut garden to see whether the pomegranate blossoms had opened there." "<b>Third reading</b><br>And the Shekhinah said: \"To the walnut garden I went down.\" The meaning is that I ascended on high until the vine blooms, and the matter is until Israel, who are likened to a vine, do deeds [good and] pleasant. And the matter of אגוז [is that its fruit is hidden], meaning that His signs are not seen in exile. And do not wonder that it says ירדתי (\"I went down\"), for behold we find וירדתי על ההרים (\"and I came down upon the mountains\")."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {4 "Bracketed text [שהוא פריו גנוז] and [טובים ו] appear in the source as marginal/interpolated glosses, marked with brackets; translated as integral to the sentence."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:51.218675616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}